  "title": "Fix the Extensible Plugin Host",
  "diagramType": "class",
  "type": "fix",
  "difficulty": "Advanced",
  "category": "Class Architecture",
  "scenario": "A CMS loads plugins through a Plugin interface, owns a dedicated PluginConfig per host instance, and lets plugins log via a Logger interface. The current diagram breaks Dependency Inversion and ownership rules: one Realization arrow is reversed, PluginHost bypasses the Plugin interface to depend directly on AuthPlugin, and PluginConfig is marked as shared Aggregation.",
  "goal": "Repair the three architectural defects so PluginHost depends only on abstractions, owns its configuration exclusively, and both plugins cleanly implement Plugin.",
  "learningObjective": "Enforce the Dependency Inversion Principle and spot reversed realization and weak ownership bugs in a multi-class diagram.",
  "hints": [
    "Realization arrows must point from concrete classes (MarkdownPlugin, AuthPlugin) toward the Plugin interface.",
    "PluginHost should depend on the Plugin interface rather than concrete AuthPlugin (Dependency Inversion).",
    "Change PluginHost -> PluginConfig from Aggregation to Composition, reverse Plugin -> MarkdownPlugin to MarkdownPlugin -> Plugin, and rewire PluginHost -> AuthPlugin to PluginHost -> Plugin."
  ],
  "solutionExplanation": "Both MarkdownPlugin and AuthPlugin realize Plugin. PluginHost depends on the Plugin interface (not concrete AuthPlugin) and composes its own PluginConfig. AuthPlugin continues to depend on Logger.",
